### 1.1 The decisive quotation — CONFIRMED

`docs/compiler-policy-interfaces.md:171-173` reads, verbatim:

> "A nil-merge with a required final result passes an **optional intermediate destination**
> to the optional left operand's conversion. It then combines that result with the converted
> fallback. A required fallback establishes a required normal result."

I diffed the ruling's quotation character-for-character against the file: exact, including
the bold markup. Section title `:169` is "Composition owns intermediate requirements"; the
closing prohibition `:176-177` is "The conversion planner must never accept an optional
result against a required destination by silently assuming some later caller will reconcile
it." The context **supports** the ruling's reading rather than qualifying it: `:174-175`
("A nullable fallback leaves the result nullable unless valid control-flow evidence removes
every absent outcome") confirms the sentence is conditioned on the *final result*, and
`:176-177` prohibits what the planner would do — not what the composition does when it
*changes the destination presented*. The companion texts check out verbatim too:
`j-prepared-value-design.md:135-137` and `swift-checkpoint-todo.md:39-42` (SW04) — both
reproduced exactly in `evidence/06-doc-citations.txt`.

